// `slice(from, to)` is `substr`'s contract on a collection: a fresh
// collection of the same kind, half-open, and out of bounds traps.
//
// Before this, taking any sub-range of a `List` needed a loop -- or, for the
// one case every program meets, `os.args()` without the program's own name,
// a `remove_at(0)` that moves every element.
List<int> xs = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50];
List<int> mid = xs.slice(1, 4);
print(mid.size());
print(mid[0]);
print(mid[2]);
// Half-open, so an empty slice is legal and `to - from` is the size.
print(xs.slice(2, 2).size());
print(xs.slice(0, 5).size());
// A fresh list: changing it leaves the original alone.
mid.push(99);
print(xs.size());
print(mid.size());
// The elements are shared, and a reference element is retained.
List<str> ws = ["a", "b", "c"];
List<str> tail = ws.slice(1, 3);
print(tail.join("-"));
ws.clear();
print(tail.join("-"));
// An Array answers with an Array, because that is the kind it is.
Array<int> a = [1, 2, 3, 4];
Array<int> part = a.slice(1, 3);
print(part.size());
print(part[1]);
// The command line without the program's own name, in one call.
List<str> argv = ["prog", "-v", "file"];
print(argv.slice(1, argv.size()).join(" "));
